    "17592186405206": {
      "course/code": "MTH 341",
      "course/description": "Linear Algebra I introduces the computational and conceptual study of linear systems, matrices, and subspaces. The course connects methods for solving equations with the structure of linear transformations and vector spaces. Determinants, linear independence, bases, dimension, and introductory eigenvalue analysis provide complementary ways to understand how a linear system or transformation behaves.",
      "course/id": {
        "$uuid": "3ace2150-672f-50e7-9c2c-027b6d4272d7"
      },
      "course/outcomes": [
        17592186406537,
        17592186406538,
        17592186406539,
        17592186406540,
        17592186406541,
        17592186406542,
        17592186406543,
        17592186406544,
        17592186406545,
        17592186406546,
        17592186406547,
        17592186406548
      ],
      "course/overview": "Work with linear systems and Gaussian elimination establishes a foundation for matrix operations, invertibility, and determinants. The course then connects these calculations with linear independence, spanning sets, subspaces, bases, and dimension. Row, column, and null spaces describe important features of a matrix, while rank–nullity relates those spaces to the structure of a system’s solutions.\n\nMatrix representations make linear transformations concrete through examples such as rotation, reflection, projection, and scaling. Eigenvalues and eigenvectors introduce directions preserved by a transformation, including examples with complex eigenvalues. The archived section used here also introduces diagonalization and illustrates eigenvalue methods through simple systems of differential equations.",
      "course/school": "Oregon State University",
      "course/title": "Linear Algebra I",
      "db/id": 17592186405206
    },
